 # Froxel Volumetric Fog with Precomputed Lighting
 
-Also called: Froxel Fog, Froxel Volumetric Fog Renderer, Frustum Voxel Fog, Volumetric Fog System
+Also called: Froxel Volumetric Fog, Froxel Fog, Frustum Voxel Fog
 
 ## Overview
-Froxel Volumetric Fog with Precomputed Lighting is a real-time rendering technique that simulates light scattering in participating media (mist, smoke, atmospheric fog) by dividing the camera view frustum into a 3D grid of voxels called "froxels" [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike traditional distance fog, which applies a uniform screen-space blur, this method allows for dynamic light beams, shadowed fog, and density variations within the volume [S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its sibling method, [Froxel Volumetric Fog with Path-Traced Shadows](2314-froxel-volumetric-fog-with-path-traced-shadows.md), by using precomputed lighting passes (specifically Next Event Estimation approximations via compute shaders) rather than runtime path tracing for shadow calculation, offering a balance between visual fidelity and performance [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+Froxel Volumetric Fog with Precomputed Lighting is a real-time rendering technique that simulates light scattering in participating media by precomputing in-scattered radiance and optical depth in a camera-aligned 3D grid (froxels) [S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of full ray-marched volumetric lighting by decoupling the expensive light transport calculation (performed once per frame in a compute shader) from the per-pixel integration (a simple front-to-back accumulation) [S3, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Froxel Volumetric Fog with Path-Traced Shadows](2314-froxel-volumetric-fog-with-path-traced-shadows.md), which uses Next Event Estimation for shadows, this variant typically relies on shadow maps or atlas-based occlusion for the precomputation step, offering a balance between visual fidelity and frame budget [S2, unofficial].
 
